In Java multithreading, await() and signal() are methods of the Condition interface,
introduced in java.util.concurrent.locks. They work with Lock objects as a more flexible
and powerful alternative to the traditional wait() / notify() mechanism.

Key Differences from wait() / notify()

Feature wait() / notify() await() / signal()
Associated with synchronized blocks / Object monitor Lock objects & Condition
Multiple wait sets One per monitor Multiple Conditions per lock
Interruptibility wait() can be interrupted await() has interruptible & uninterruptible variants
Timeout support wait(long timeout) await(long time, TimeUnit unit)
Fairness No Depends on Lock implementation (e.g., ReentrantLock(true))

Basic Usage Pattern

```java
Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
Condition condition = lock.newCondition();

// Thread 1 (waiter)
lock.lock();
try {
    while (!conditionMet()) {
        condition.await();  // releases lock and waits
    }
    // proceed
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}

// Thread 2 (signaler)
lock.lock();
try {
    // change state
    condition.signal();   // or signalAll()
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}
```

Important Rules

1. Always call await()/signal() while holding the associated Lock – otherwise 
IllegalMonitorStateException.
2. Always use a loop around await() to guard against spurious wakeups.
3. signal() wakes one waiting thread; signalAll() wakes all.
4. await() releases the lock atomically and reacquires it before returning.

Example: Bounded Queue

```java
public class BoundedQueue<T> {
    private final Queue<T> queue = new LinkedList<>();
    private final int capacity;
    private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();
    private final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();

    public BoundedQueue(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; }

    public void put(T item) throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (queue.size() == capacity) {
                notFull.await();
            }
            queue.add(item);
            notEmpty.signal();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public T take() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            while (queue.isEmpty()) {
                notEmpty.await();
            }
            T item = queue.poll();
            notFull.signal();
            return item;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

Advanced Variants

· awaitUninterruptibly() – ignores interruption until after waiting.
· await(long time, TimeUnit unit) – returns false if timeout occurs.
· awaitUntil(Date deadline) – waits until an absolute point in time.

When to Use await()/signal() over wait()/notify()?

· Need multiple wait queues (e.g., separate conditions for “not full” and “not empty”).
· Want more flexible locking (e.g., ReadWriteLock, try-lock, fair locking).
· Need interruptible waits with timeout or custom scheduling.

await()/signal() are not a replacement for simple cases, but they give you cleaner, safer,
and more scalable coordination when you outgrow synchronized.
